  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO \nCOUNTIES...\n\nLight offshore flow has been observed late this morning, with \nhighest gusts across the San Diego County mts with gusts up to 35 \nMPH. Winds will be easing into the afternoon as a weak ridge drifts \nover the Desert Southwest. Similar temperatures can be expected into \nSunday with greater onshore flow. A weak shortwave will cross over \nthe region later on Sunday into early Monday bringing the chance for \nlight precipitation, first at the coast and then pushing into the \ncoastal slopes. Models are in fair agreement about light rain \nstarting for areas near the coast sometime around the sunset hour,\na bit later further inland. The forecast remains on track for \nlight accumulations overall with one tenth of an inch to no rain \nat all, locally higher in heavier showers. Though snow levels will\nbe near 5,000 feet, the presence of relatively drier air will \nmake it difficult for much accumulation. Most areas above 5,000 \nwill see a dusting to near one inch for higher mountains. \nPrecipitation will end from the north to south on Monday morning \nwith more sunshine by the afternoon.\n\nAnother weak ridge tries to develop on Tuesday, where cooler \ntemperatures remain under mostly sunny skies. At the same time, a \nstronger low pressure system will move into the the Pacific \nNorthwest coastline. As it does so, models indicate a shortwave will \nbreak off from the main flow of the system and drift southward \nacross California. Most model consensus shows this system becoming \nweaker, and not making it very far south. This evidence is also \nshown in a drying trend that has been noticed for Wednesday, where \nprecipitation chances have become more limited. Confidence is \nincreasing on a dry day for many with temperatures a degree or two \nwarmer from Tuesday. \n\nModel consensus on what becomes of the system mentioned above is \nstill in somewhat of question. Confidence continues to be high that \nThanksgiving Day will be dry and calm. Most models bring an \nexpanding ridge from the south over the region, keeping the \nWednesday weather system away from the region by Thanksgiving and \nbeyond. If this occurs, relatively quiet weather with somewhat \nwarmer weather and weak offshore flow along the coastal slopes. Some \nmember of the GFS show a more outlier pattern, with the weather \nsystem forming into a closed low over Arizona. If this occurs, the \npressure gradient would tighten, leading to more elevated Santa Ana \nwinds into the valleys with even warmer weather. NBM for now shows \nhighs mainly in the 70s and lower 80s for the valleys west of the \nmountains. Yet another area of low pressure moves closer to the West \nCoast by next weekend. This will bring a cooling trend, but where \nthis system goes and if it will bring any additional precipitation \nto our region is still uncertain.",
